<h2>Why 16 Is the Highest-Leverage Training Age</h2>
<p>Sixteen sits at a unique intersection: most athletes are at or near the tail end of their adolescent growth spurt, which means natural speed gains are still coming on their own — and there's still enough runway before junior and senior year evaluations to convert dedicated training into a genuinely different number. Nike, Under Armour, and Rivals camps have started tracking sophomore standouts, but the pressure of "this number is final" hasn't arrived yet the way it has at 17.</p>
<h3>Hand-Timed vs Electronic — The 16-Year-Old Reality</h3>
<p>At 16, most of your times will come from school-run combines, summer camps, or informal testing — a mix of hand-timed and electronic. Hand-timed results are typically 0.2 to 0.3 seconds faster than electronically timed equivalents. If you're comparing your number to a friend's or to a benchmark table, always check whether both numbers were captured the same way. A 4.6 hand-timed and a 4.6 electronic are not remotely the same level of speed.</p>
<h3>How Much Development Remains at 16</h3>
<p>This is the single biggest advantage of testing and training at 16: more development remains here than at any other point in high school. Natural physical maturation alone typically accounts for 0.15 to 0.3 seconds of improvement between 16 and 18. Layer dedicated speed training on top of that window, and a 16-year-old running 5.0 today has a completely realistic path to the mid-4.6s to low 4.7s range by senior year — a jump that gets progressively harder to replicate the older you get.</p>

